{\b0 \cf31 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf31 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Background
\par 
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
\u160\'3f
\par 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
The UCP is a set of contractual rules published by the 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/2-107-6724?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=DocumentItem&contextData=(sc.Default)" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
International Chamber of Commerce (ICC)
}}}
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
. It is a voluntary code which does not have the force of law. The purpose of the UCP is to standardise banking practice in the worldwide operation of letters of credit. It is a code of rules which enables letters of credit to be routinely dealt with by banks across the world on a common basis, and is therefore \u8220\'3ffundamental\u8221\'3f to the acceptability of letters of credit in international commerce: 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/D-101-7512?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=PLDocumentLink&contextData=(sc.Default)" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Taurus Petroleum Ltd v State Oil Marketing Co of the Ministry of Oil, Iraq [2017] UKSC 64; [2018] AC 690 [61]
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
.
\par 
}
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
\u160\'3f
\par 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
The parties to a credit must agree to incorporate the UCP into the credit for the rules to be binding upon them. This is achieved by an express term in the credit that provides it is issued subject to the terms of the UCP. For background information on credits and the UCP, see 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/1-107-3740?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=DocumentItem&contextData=(sc.Default)" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Practice note, Letters of credit: overview
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 and in particular the sections 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/1-107-3740?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=DocumentItem&contextData=(sc.Default)#co_anchor_a1003334" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
What is a letter of credit?
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 and 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/1-107-3740?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=DocumentItem&contextData=(sc.Default)#co_anchor_a1003341" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
The UCP
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
.
\par 
}
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
\u160\'3f
\par 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
The UCP is updated from time to time to cover technical developments, to incorporate ICC decisions on disputes under the rules and to try to undo any unwelcome consequences of litigation. The current version of the UCP, UCP 600, \u8220\'3fcame into effect\u8221\'3f on 1 July 2007 when it was approved by the ICC for use from that date and this was also the date on which provision could be made within the relevant 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/2-107-7356?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=DocumentItem&contextData=(sc.Default)" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
SWIFT
}}}
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
 message formats for its application (most credits are issued by SWIFT, for more information, see 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/1-107-3740?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=DocumentItem&contextData=(sc.Default)#co_anchor_a1003383" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Practice note, Letters of credit: overview: Method of issue
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
). Although UCP 600 supersedes UCP 500, UCP 500 remains relevant to credits issued under UCP 500, to the extent that such credits remain in operation or are subject to litigation or arbitration proceedings.
\par 
}
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
\u160\'3f
\par 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
The eUCP supplements UCP 600 to accommodate presentation of electronic records alone or in combination with paper documents, in circumstances where the eUCP are expressly incorporated. The current version of the eUCP is eUCP version 2.0, which came into effect on 1 July 2019. The eUCP rules have been intentionally developed with a version number in order that they can be updated regularly without impacting upon other existing ICC rules, thereby reducing the time required to develop any potential identified revision. Thus, an eUCP credit must indicate the applicable version of the eUCP.
\par 
}
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
\u160\'3f
\par 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
For more information on the eUCP, see 
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i1 \fs20 
{\field {\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://uk.practicallaw.thomsonreuters.com/1-107-3740?originationContext=document&vr=3.0&rs=PLUK1.0&transitionType=DocumentItem&contextData=(sc.Default)#co_anchor_a1003503" }{\fldrslt 
{\b0 \cf23 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i1 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
Practice note, Letters of credit: overview: New technology: e-commerce and the eUCP
}}}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
.
\par 
}
}
}
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ri0 \i0 \qj \fs20 \li0 
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs20 \sa0 \sb0 
\u160\'3f
}
{\b0 \cf21 \f2 \i0 \fs20 
